WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.750
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:08.240
On 17th March 1930 construction started on
what would be the tallest building in the

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.790
world, the Empire State Building and so the
global battle of which country could erect

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.289
the biggest structure began.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:23.849
America managed two more erections and held
onto its trophy for an impressive 67 years.

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:30.269
But then Kuala Lumpur came swooping in to
take the title with a mighty double erection,

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:32.430
the Petronas Towers.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:39.680
Taiwan stole it from them in 2004 with Taipei
101 and finally in 2010 the United Arab Emirates

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.420
finished construction of the insanely tall
Burj Khalifa, taking the crown with an impressive

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.292
62% height increase on Taipei 101.

00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:54.040
And right now in a room somewhere a group
of architects hired for their shared love

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:00.480
of designing stupidly tall buildings are finishing
up the plans for what will be the next monolithic

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.159
megastructure to take its place amongst the
clouds.

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:13.430
Scheduled for completion in 2020, Jeddah tower
will be the world’s first 1 kilometre tall

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.850
structure, this thing will be so mind-bogglingly
tall, they may as well have named it “compensation

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:19.850
tower”.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:26.569
But all these incredible feats of architecture
and engineering have something in common.

00:01:26.569 --> 00:01:34.249
They are all based on a set of rules, rules
that are so vital that these buildings would

00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:39.759
never have been possible without them, neither
would many of the other buildings you see

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:42.219
around you every day.

00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:45.450
These rules are the rules of geometry.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.969
These ground rules for the way that shape
and space interact with each other, were created

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:57.109
over 2,300 years ago by a Greek mathematician
named Euclid.

00:01:57.109 --> 00:02:01.109
Euclid wrote a series of 13 books which he
named The Elements.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:07.600
They created many of the foundations of number
theory and geometry that mathematicians still

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.010
use today.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:15.530
It is said that Euclid’s Elements are the
most studied books in the world, after The

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:21.350
Bible, another book that is really obsessive
about rules, although it seems rules about

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:28.520
what one shall or shall not do to thy neighbour’s
ox are more popular than shapes and numbers.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:35.160
Euclid’s principles of geometry have without
a doubt changed the world, the geometry that

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.700
Euclid founded is used in thousands of modern
day applications from computer graphics to

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:46.110
car and aircraft design and the highly unique
shape of Donald Trump’s hair.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:53.000
Euclid studied, researched and wrote his 13
books in a very, very special place.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.010
A place that held every secret of the ancient
world, a true temple of knowledge.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.010
It was called the Library of Alexandria.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:09.180
Built under the reign of Alexander the Great,
In the ancient port of Alexandria in Egypt,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.230
stood a monolith of knowledge and wisdom.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:18.879
The Library of Alexandria was built on the
banks of the Mediterranean sea in 288 BC.

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:22.520
It was a structure of epic proportions.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:28.300
Historians believe the library contained reading
rooms, meeting rooms, gardens, lecture halls,

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:34.900
a shared dining room, an acquisitions department
and a cataloging department, thankfully human

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.740
resources hadn’t been created yet.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.040
The Library of Alexandria wasn’t just a
place to come to read scrolls to brush up

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:48.370
on your knowledge or maybe to rent out a blue
scroll of Aphrodite getting it on with Hades,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.710
it was also the world’s finest research
facility.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:59.380
It was in many ways the earliest example of
a fully-functioning university in all of history.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:05.320
Scholars travelled from all over Ancient Greece
and beyond to study and carry out research

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.710
at the Library of Alexandria.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.930
Names such as Euclid, one of the greatest
mathematicians of all time graced the halls

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.100
and rooms of this once great place.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:22.410
As well as Eratosthenes a Greek Mathematician
who was over 2,000 years ahead of his time.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:30.090
In 1957 Sputnik was launched, the first manmade
satellite to be launched into space, we have

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:35.250
since sent many more satellites to forever
orbit our planet.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.130
Today there are around 1,100 lumps of metal
orbiting our planet, collectively costing

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.139
many billions of pounds to launch and maintain.

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:50.880
One of the major reasons we began launching
satellites into space was to answer a simple

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:57.389
question, what is the circumference of the
Earth, how big is this huge lump of rock we

00:04:57.389 --> 00:04:58.660
live on?

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:04.290
Until we had an incredibly accurate measurement
of the Earth, technologies such as GPS were

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:05.290
impossible.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:11.040
But over 2,000 years ago an ancient greek
mathematician who studied and worked at the

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:18.410
Library of Alexandria, stuck a stick in the
ground at Alexandria and worked out the circumference

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:19.740
of the Earth.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.450
Eratosthenes was his name and he didn’t
need billion dollar satellites, just fairly

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.760
basic maths.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.040
Using the distance between Alexandria and
the nearby town of Syene and the shadow cast

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.010
on a stick he placed in the ground, he used
mathematics to conclude that the Earth’s

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:42.290
circumference is 40,000km.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:48.310
And guess what we found out after spending
billions of pounds on space technology 2,000

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:54.760
years later, we discovered that the Earth’s
circumference is about 40,075 km.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:06:04.880
That’s right, Eratosthenes was out by just
75km, that’s almost negligible.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.600
Well done man with stick, very impressive.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:14.560
This is just the tip of the many multitude
of incredible discoveries, studies and experiments

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.669
conducted at the Library of Alexandria.

00:06:16.669 --> 00:06:23.940
But sadly, we cannot see beyond the tip of
this iceberg of knowledge and discovery, for

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.840
it is all lost.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.620
You see, The Library of Alexandria was burned
down.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:36.411
If you ask any historian which historical
moment they mourn the most, the day the Library

00:06:36.411 --> 00:06:42.230
of Alexandria was destroyed will likely be
at the top of their list, right alongside

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:46.610
the moment George Lucas decided to make the
Star Wars prequels.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:53.280
It is thought that if the Library of Alexandria
had never burnt down, the progress of humanity

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.020
would be 1,000 years ahead of what it is now.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.729
The Library of Alexandria contained a lot
of scrolls.

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:08.830
It is estimated there were around 700,000
scrolls stored within the walls of this once

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.000
great place.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.419
These scrolls would have held almost all the
secrets of the ancient world and filled numerous

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:18.730
gaps of historical knowledge that we have
today.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:23.270
They covered every topic from maths to medicine,
science to history.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:28.199
The incredible things we could have learnt
from this library boggles the mind.

00:07:28.199 --> 00:07:36.610
But the way the Library gathered such an incredible
collection of knowledge is somewhat barbaric.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:43.580
The very purpose of the Library of Alexandria’s
existence, the reason it was built, was to

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:48.139
collect and catalogue all the world’s knowledge
in one place.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:54.160
You see the city of Alexandria was a trading
port and every day hundreds of ships would

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.280
dock in its harbour to conduct trade.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.770
It was mandatory that every ship that docked
in Alexandria would be searched from bow to

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.740
stern, by the authorities upon arrival.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:12.050
But they weren't looking for gold and treasures,
oh no, they were looking for scrolls and any

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.810
other bits of knowledge they could find.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:21.620
Any scrolls would be seized and taken to the
Library where they would be hand copied, word

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:27.729
for word and then the original would be stored
in the Library of Alexandria and only the

00:08:27.729 --> 00:08:42.400
copy would be returned back to the owner of
the ship.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.380
On a royal mandate library officials were
also tasked with traveling to all the markets

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:54.190
and book fairs from Rhodes to Athens and hoovering
up any scrolls or documents they could obtain.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:59.510
The result of all this was one of the greatest
collections of knowledge the world has ever

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:00.510
seen.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:06.279
There is no doubt that the library contained
masses of knowledge and history of the ancient

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:10.790
world that we could only dream of knowing
today.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:15.710
It was the ancient version of the Vatican
Secret Archives but many times grander in

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:20.570
scale and content, also quite a bit less secret.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:25.770
But then one day a man in a toga came along
and accidentally burnt this great library

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.390
to the ground.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:33.040
How do you “accidentally” burn down a
humongous library you may be thinking, well

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.470
that’s a very good question.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:39.750
That silly man in a toga was Julius Caesar.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:44.930
Caesar was an obsessive conqueror, on an average
day he conquered at least two cities before

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.700
his morning muesli.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:51.290
So the seat of knowledge within the Greek
Empire that was the port of Alexandria was

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:54.700
a lovely looking cherry sat atop his world
map.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:58.770
And it was juicy enough for Caesar to attempt
an invasion.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:05.470
But in 48 BCE when he rocked up at Alexandria’s
harbour with his small fleet of roman ships

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:12.480
he knew that the Egyptian fleet docked at
Alexandria were far greater in number than

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.220
what he had brought with him.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:20.050
So in true Julius Caesar style, he had his
troops set alight to them all and burned all

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:23.690
the egyptian ships to smithereens.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:29.810
He only intended to burn the ships but the
fire got out of control and it quickly spread

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:34.500
to the Royal Quarter of Alexandria, where
the Library was.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:40.210
The fire soon engulfed a large section of
the great library, forever destroying many

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:45.980
thousands of scrolls, it’s estimated that
after the fire the collection went down from

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:50.760
700,000 scrolls to around 200,000.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:56.200
Julius Caesar was a great admirer of books
and literature, historians insist he would

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:01.480
have been rather remorseful that his fire
had inadvertently destroyed part of the greatest

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:03.530
library of all time.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:09.630
But Caesar can’t take all of the infamy
for this great institute’s eventual demise.

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:13.500
After the fire there were a number of efforts
to keep the library going, it was extended

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:22.660
and moved but due to a succession of wars
and invaders it was eventually destroyed piece-by-piece

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:28.670
and sadly so was almost every piece of literature
within its walls.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:33.830
Throughout its time the Library was actually
set fire to three times, it was getting to

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:39.029
the point where they should have started taking
bets on when it would next burst into flames.

00:11:39.029 --> 00:11:45.800
Due to mixed reports of the events, historians
aren’t exactly sure who finished off the

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.440
library after Caesars accidental fire, but
three names continuously pop up throughout

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.190
textbooks as potential suspects.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:12:01.120
The first suspect is Roman soldier Tenagino
Probus under the orders of Roman Emperor Aurelian

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:10.150
in 270 CE, the second suspect is Theophilus,
the pope of Alexandria in 391 CE as part of

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:15.790
a Christian crusade to destroy scientific
literature, because you know, science has

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:21.080
caused so many wars and endless suffering
worldwide, religion has never been responsible

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:23.240
for anything like that.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:31.050
And finally in 640 CE the once great but now
dying library was reportedly finished off

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:37.740
by Caliph Umar when his muslim army captured
Alexandria and the rest of Egypt.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:42.730
It is written that when he captured the city
he said “If those books are in agreement

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:49.180
with the Quran, we have no need of them; and
if these are opposed to the Quran, destroy

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:50.180
them.”

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:55.870
It has been said that the destruction of the
Library of Alexandria set human progress back

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.330
by 1,000 years.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:04.010
If it had survived we could today be living
in a world of self-driving flying cars, frequent

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:09.890
space travel, superhuman AI and a knife that
toasts bread as you slice it.

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:14.830
There has been much debate over this claim
and it may be quite exaggerated, but whether

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:20.240
the loss of what was once the greatest library
in the world really did hinder human progress

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:28.300
or not, the fact remains that it was one of
the greatest tragedies of all of history,

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:35.839
and there are many historians who would kill
to read just 5% of the scrolls it contained.

00:13:35.839 --> 00:13:39.430
Well historians aren’t really known for
their killings, perhaps I should rephrase

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:43.960
that to there are many who would pay a very
reasonable amount of money.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:48.860
But as with all tragedies there is a lesson
to be learnt.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:54.880
Knowledge is humanity’s single greatest
achievement, scientific achievements have

00:13:54.880 --> 00:14:00.670
saved millions of lives and every day, knowledge
is built upon all around the world and is

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:07.709
used to keep us all safer, provide transport,
food and water to those who need it most.

00:14:07.709 --> 00:14:56.430
So as a society may we never let another book
burn, unless it’s Fifty Shades 

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:06.070
of Grey.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:40.839
Thanks for watching.

